<!-- CHAPTER 12 -->
<div class="page-break"></div>
<h2>12. Pericyclic Hub & Woodward-Hoffmann FMO Engine</h2>
<p>
  Pericyclic reactions are notoriously tricky in competitive exams like CSIR-NET and GATE. ChemBuddy incorporates a Frontier Molecular Orbital (FMO) engine:
</p>

<h3>Woodward-Hoffmann Selection Rules Matrix</h3>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Reaction Type</th>
      <th>Electron System</th>
      <th>Thermal ($\Delta$)</th>
      <th>Photochemical ($h
u$)</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td rowspan="2"><strong>Electrocyclic Reactions</strong></td>
      <td>$4n$ electrons (e.g. Butadiene)</td>
      <td><strong>Conrotatory</strong></td>
      <td><strong>Disrotatory</strong></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>$4n+2$ electrons (e.g. Hexatriene)</td>
      <td><strong>Disrotatory</strong></td>
      <td><strong>Conrotatory</strong></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td rowspan="2"><strong>Cycloadditions</strong></td>
      <td>$4n$ electrons (e.g. $[2+2]$)</td>
      <td>Symmetry Forbidden ($s+s$)</td>
      <td><strong>Allowed</strong> ($s+s$)</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>$4n+2$ electrons (e.g. $[4+2]$ Diels-Alder)</td>
      <td><strong>Allowed</strong> ($s+s$, Endo selective)</td>
      <td>Symmetry Forbidden</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td rowspan="2"><strong>Sigmatropic Shifts</strong></td>
      <td>$[1,3]$ hydrogen shift</td>
      <td>Forbidden (Suprafacial)</td>
      <td><strong>Allowed</strong> (Suprafacial)</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>$[1,5]$ / $[3,3]$ (Cope, Claisen)</td>
      <td><strong>Allowed</strong> (Suprafacial / Chair-like)</td>
      <td>Forbidden</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<!-- CHAPTER 13 -->
<h2>13. Smart Flashcards & Spaced Repetition (Active Recall)</h2>
<p>
  ChemBuddy implements the <strong>SuperMemo-2 (SM-2) spaced repetition algorithm</strong> to ensure long-term memorization of chemical reactions, reagents, named mechanisms, and spectroscopic tables.
</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Active Recall Testing:</strong> Flashcards present the prompt/reactant first. You mentally formulate the product or mechanism before tapping to reveal the answer.</li>
  <li><strong>Rating System:</strong> Rate your recall quality from 1 to 4:
    <ul>
      <li><code>Again (1)</code>: Complete blackout. Card repeats within the same session.</li>
      <li><code>Hard (2)</code>: Recalled with significant difficulty. Interval expands slowly.</li>
      <li><code>Good (3)</code>: Correct recall with normal hesitation. Optimal interval expansion.</li>
      <li><code>Easy (4)</code>: Instant, confident recall. Interval expands aggressively.</li>
    </ul>
  </li>
  <li><strong>Automated Deck Generation:</strong> Automatically generates flashcard sets from your uploaded PDF notes.</li>
</ul>

<!-- CHAPTER 14 -->
<div class="page-break"></div>
<h2>14. PDF Library, Study Hub & Grounded RAG AI Assistant</h2>
<p>
  The PDF Library allows you to store, annotate, and interrogate your course materials.
</p>

<h3>Strict Single-Source-of-Truth RAG Architecture</h3>
<p>
  Most AI chat tools hallucinate when asked advanced chemistry questions. ChemBuddy's RAG engine adheres to strict grounding rules:
</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Strictly Grounded on Your Notes:</strong> When chatting in the PDF Study Hub, the AI is constrained strictly to the text extracted from your uploaded PDF.</li>
  <li><strong>Standardized Refusal:</strong> If a topic is not present in your course PDF, the assistant explicitly states: <em>"This topic is not covered in the provided document."</em> rather than guessing.</li>
  <li><strong>8192 Token Complete Answers:</strong> Output limits have been quadrupled to 8192 tokens. Answers are comprehensive, complete, and formatted with KaTeX math and structural ASCII schemes.</li>
</ul>

<!-- CHAPTER 15 -->
<h2>15. Exam Mode & BCU Model Answer Generator</h2>
<p>
  Designed around Bangalore City University (BCU) and Indian central university examination patterns:
</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>2-Mark Question Mode:</strong> Concise, precise definitions, IUPAC nomenclature, and brief statements.</li>
  <li><strong>5-Mark Question Mode:</strong> Step-by-step mechanism, electron arrow pushing, and key intermediates.</li>
  <li><strong>10-Mark Question Mode:</strong> Comprehensive essays, retro-synthetic analysis, multi-step synthesis, and spectroscopic validation.</li>
</ul>

